  "body": "Follow-up to #61.\n\nThe arms landed with a host deadline around each mutant run. Both harnesses already bound their own waits in **DUT cycles** \u2014 the gasket harness so that a wedged gasket fails the suite instead of hanging it (the very property the first gasket mutant exercises), and `tsngen_main.cpp` with an explicit tick ceiling on every wait.\n\nSo the deadline answered a question the DUT already answers, and answered it in a machine-dependent way: the same mutant could be a catch on one host and a `TIMEOUT` on a slower one. A mutation arm whose verdict depends on machine speed is not evidence about the assertions.\n\nThe runs are now plain subprocess calls; the `TIMEOUT` branch and the process-group kill machinery go with the deadline that needed them, and the reason is recorded where the verdict is read.\n\nThis also keeps `milan-fpga`'s Rule 8 wall-clock ratchet at 3 \u2014 *\"New behavioral tests use DUT cycles, not host time\"* \u2014 which the deadlines would have pushed to 5.\n\n**Evidence:** both arms pass unchanged, gasket **5/5** and tsngen **4/4**, every mutant caught and both positive controls passing.\n",
